1. The pointer itself: one word, and the niche is kept
   *const u64         8 bytes
   Box<u64>           8 bytes
   Rc<u64>            8 bytes
   Arc<u64>           8 bytes
   Weak<u64>          8 bytes
   Option<Rc<u64>>    8 bytes
   This is the zero-cost part: the handle is no bigger than the raw
   pointer you would have kept by hand.

2. Making one: an allocation, sized by what is stored beside the value
   Box::new(7u64)                     alloc 1  free 0  bytes  8
   Rc::new(7u64)                      alloc 1  free 0  bytes 24
   Arc::new(7u64)                     alloc 1  free 0  bytes 24
   String::from("hello")              alloc 1  free 0  bytes  5
   Vec::<u32>::with_capacity(4)       alloc 1  free 0  bytes 16
   Cow::Borrowed("hello")             alloc 0  free 0  bytes  0
   RefCell::new(5i32)                 alloc 0  free 0  bytes  0
   Box asks for the u64 alone. Rc and Arc ask for 24: a strong count,
   a weak count, then the u64. The two that allocate nothing are the
   two that store nothing on the heap.

3. Sharing one: no allocation, but a count changes at run time
   Rc::clone(&shared)                 alloc 0  free 0  bytes  0
   Rc::downgrade(&shared)             alloc 0  free 0  bytes  0
   strong_count 2   weak_count 1

4. Letting go: only the last strong owner drops the value
   drop(second)                       alloc 0  free 0  bytes  0
   strong_count 1
   drop(shared), the last Rc          alloc 0  free 0  bytes  0
   weak.upgrade() None
   drop(weak), the last Weak          alloc 0  free 1  bytes  0
   The value was dropped with the last Rc, but its 24 bytes were freed
   only with the last Weak, because the weak count lives there too.

5. RefCell: a flag in the value, and a check on every borrow
   size_of i32 4   Cell<i32> 4   RefCell<i32> 16
   borrow_mut while a borrow is live: Err(BorrowMutError)
   after the borrow ends:             Ok(())
   Cell hands out no references, so it needs no flag. RefCell counts
   its borrows in a word beside the value and checks it at run time.
